[Event "Jobava London vs ...d5"]

{The Jobava London (1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 + Bf4) is the aggressive cousin of the London. The knight on c3 eyes b5, and White can castle long and push the kingside pawns.}
1. d4 {Centre. The pawn on d4 controls e5.}
1... d5 {Symmetry. The pawn on d5 controls e4.}
2. Nc3 {[%cal Gc3b5] The Jobava: the knight eyes b5. The knight on c3 eyes the pawn on d5 and controls e4.}
2... Nf6 {Develops. The knight on f6 controls e4.}
3. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4c7] The bishop eyes c7. The bishop on f4 eyes the pawn on c7 and controls e5.}
3... e6 {Solid.}
 (3... Bf5 {Mirrors the bishop. The bishop on f5 eyes the pawn on c2 and controls e4.}
4. f3 {Prepares e4 or g4. The pawn on f3 controls e4.}
4... e6 {Solid.}
5. g4 {[%cal Gg4f5] Hunts the bishop. The pawn on g4 eyes the bishop on f5.}
5... Bg6 {Retreats. The bishop on g6 eyes the pawn on c2 and controls e4.}
6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5.}
6... h6 {Makes room.}
7. e3 {Solid.}
)
 (3... c5 {Counters at once. The pawn on c5 eyes the pawn on d4.}
4. e3 {Solid.}
4... Nc6 {Develops. The knight on c6 eyes the pawn on d4 and controls e5.}
5. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7] Threatens Nc7+. The knight on b5 eyes the pawn on a7.}
)
4. e3 {Solid.}
4... Bd6 {Offers a trade. The bishop on d6 eyes the bishop on f4 and controls e5.}
5. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5d6] Wins the bishop pair. The knight on b5 eyes the pawn on a7 and the pawn on c7.} *

[Event "Jobava London vs King's Indian setups"]

{Against ...g6, White pushes h4.}
1. d4 {Centre. The pawn on d4 controls e5.}
1... Nf6 {Flexible. The knight on f6 controls d5 and e4.}
2. Nc3 {Jobava. The knight on c3 controls d5 and e4.}
2... g6 {Fianchetto.}
3. Bf4 {Develops. The bishop on f4 eyes the pawn on c7 and controls e5.}
3... Bg7 {Fianchetto.}
4. e3 {Solid.}
4... d6 {Solid. The pawn on d6 controls e5.}
 (4... O-O {Castles early. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Attacks the castled king right away.}
)
5. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] The kingside push.} *
